import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base lang="en" title="Musings"><article class="prose"><p class="label">Writing</p><h1>Musings.</h1><p>Loose, occasional writing that doesn't belong to any collection.</p><div class="ep-layout"><div class="ep-tabs"><button class="ep-tab active" data-ep="0">01 · Graduation Reflections - Preface</button></div><div class="ep-panels"><div class="ep-panel active" data-ep="0"><p>The day has finally come. You sit in front of the computer and open that painfully familiar document, the one you've revised more times than you can count.</p><p>You've lost count of how many times you've collapsed in front of the screen crying, but when you open your blurred eyes, what meets them is still the same familiar, blinking cursor.</p><p>At your most useless, you find yourself needing ChatGPT to comfort you.</p><p>Where should the story start? Maybe three years ago. Looking back now, you stand at this end and look at the road you've walked, a thousand feelings rushing up at once, and in the end all you get is a bitter laugh.</p><p>You knew this day would come eventually, but you're still not sure you're ready for it. Having said goodbye to student life, you know clearly that you are no longer the person who once crouched in Hong Kong airport, secretly crying while eating an egg sandwich three years ago. Now you handle things that look thorny with ease, yet you fall apart over the smallest things. "Parting" — whether it's the parting of the living or the parting of death. So many familiar things feel like they could vanish the very next second.</p><p class="scene-break">· · ·</p><p>Take the time you finally managed to get back to Toronto — even though it had only been two weeks, you still noticed the small changes right away. You bought a SOE at the coffee shop by the station, and the coffee, freshly bought, was knocked out of your hand by someone passing by. You listened to their string of "sorry, sorry," and, as you so often do, smiled and said "it's fine." What you didn't let on was that the coffee spilled on the ground shattered your heart into a thousand pieces in that instant. You've always cursed this city as a wreck, but you never quite understood what Toronto meant to you. All you know is that its nickname, "the city," along with another place called "the mountain city" (Montreal), kept appearing again and again in your writing. Maybe you had already become the adult you once dreamed of as a child, but you can no longer get back to that childhood purity. Over these years you figured out the price of growing too fast, so should you keep growing at all? That question has troubled you for a long time. You still haven't reached an answer. You stand in the flood of time, watching the people around you — some hurrying, some sprinting — as if you alone were held in place by an invisible shackle. You step out of the TTC subway station breathing clouds into the cold air, and find that under the familiar amber light, a thin scattering of snow has already begun to fall. You saw Westlife live in Toronto once — a kind of repayment to your childhood self. Your usual record store is on Spadina, your favourite coffee shop is on College, and the bakery you like is on Wellesley. You've always stubbornly loved going to Centre Island, where U of T students often picnic, and so many of the scenes in your own writing are drawn from there. You've seen the fireworks of summer and felt the snow-white of winter. You miss the $3.99 pineapples at the Farmboy near Harbourfront, and the place on Dundas where you used to eat with friends. What did those two-plus years in Toronto actually give you? Even you can't answer that.</p><p class="scene-break">· · ·</p><p>On Christmas Eve you sat in a McDonald's on West Nanjing Road, sipping Coke and nibbling a grilled chicken burger, staring blankly at the crowds passing below, a new cherry McFlurry and half-eaten fries on the table beside you. You realize that eight times out of ten, whenever you come to this city it's for an exam — a chance to actually take the city in slowly like this might be the first time. Still, you remember the stray cats outside SISU's Yifu building, remember the first time you put in contact lenses with shaking hands, remember the thrill of first stepping into that unknown territory. The foreign-language bookstore on Fuzhou Road is the same as ever — four years ago its top-floor ceiling leaked, and four years later it still leaks. You stood up to put your tray on the return rack, and noticed, to your surprise, that the two-seat table you'd been sitting at had already been taken by a couple. It was only then you realized the restaurant's BGM was Eason Chan's "Christmas Knot" — how fitting, you thought with a bitter laugh. You turned, pushed open the door, and stepped back out into Shanghai's winter wind. You looked closely at this city, just as gilded and indulgent as Toronto, closed your eyes, and tried to search for your own possibilities.</p><p>But in the end, what does any of that glitter and indulgence have to do with you?</p><p>You boarded a flight to New York on a whim, and only you knew that the real reason was to satisfy some unrealistic fantasy in your heart. Manhattan's spring wind couldn't blow away the mess of your thoughts. Maybe long before that you'd already decided New York would be your first stop into this country. Pain, unease, confusion, anxiety, at a loss for what to do — you stood in front of the bronze bull on Wall Street, feeling as if every rule that ran the world either had everything to do with you, or nothing at all. In that moment you found yourself envying the people who had genuinely found their own way of living. Centennial Park and all that glamour from a Taylor song tied you to this world through countless invisible threads.</p><p>"So are you sad again?"</p><p>"Yeah. I'm sad again."</p><p>Even after all these years, you still haven't grown at all. Sometimes you really do just want to hide, or just die and be done with it. That's what you think.</p><p>Whether it's the blue-eyed, blond-haired boy you'd only just met, or the blue-eyed, blond-haired girl you'd known for years, they are equally dazzling in your eyes.</p><p>"We have always longed for a miracle to fall on the people we love, but we never once believed we ourselves could deserve happiness." That's a line from something you once wrote, and even now, after all this time, you still believe it. Because you aren't strong enough, self-loathing has simply become a part of who you are.</p><p>You can feel genuinely happy — even cry — over someone else's joy, but you have never once believed you could receive that joy yourself.</p><p>You open your eyes again and find yourself already swaying inside a purple subway line carriage. The announcement is the same mechanical female voice as always; you realize you're about to transfer, so you get off. Stepping onto the green line, you lean against the crowd, close your eyes for a moment's rest, and watch the students passing by, knowing you can never go back to that era. You don't know why your connection to Heluokou runs so deep, or why Wuxi's rush hour is always this packed. When you open your eyes again, you find you've somehow been teleported to the bright yellow line. You've never quite understood why the two and four lines are the same colour in all three of the cities that matter to you. But the line that connects you and that person is still the same glaring red and radiant yellow of the number one line. This illusion leaves you constantly hazy, unsure which city you're actually standing in. Honestly, you've never known whether this feeling is good or bad. You've always been torn — drawn to Western freedom and equality, yet unable to fully shake off the East Asian yoke placed on you. You suddenly remember a professor mentioning the "rat race" in some senior-year class, and think that maybe you'd already become part of it without even realizing. You want to push forward with everything you have, but the moment you do, the sadness of the past comes flooding back like a tide. You, who never tire of the smell of fresh book ink, have never been stingy with your love and impulse for certain things — but the moment you decide to commit to them, the reality of a world that favours science over the humanities, and a bleak job market, makes you start doubting the path you chose. The humanities should never be gutted or looked down on — what they need is a revolution thorough enough to overturn the stereotype. You remember a joke you once made with a friend, right after you got your offer: "I want to marry my major." You understand, in the end, that people who write code will always earn more, and hold more status, than people who write essays — but you don't think writing a good essay is any easier than writing good code. Science builds the body; the humanities tend to the soul. And between the two, freedom of the soul has always come first for you.</p><p>You've walked the road your seniors once walked, and, without quite noticing, become someone else's senior in turn. Looking back at what you've written, you realize you actually knew the answers to some of these questions all along — you always did. Just like a line from something you once wrote: "Those beautiful qualities have been asleep in you all along. One day, when you look back, you'll find the things that once felt impossibly out of reach have already filled your soul." Standing on the rooftop of Rockefeller Center, looking up at the sky you'd hoped to see for so long, you still thought of the seniors who once helped you along the way. However many times you've said "thank you" to the people who guided you, it will never be enough.</p><p>You remember, on the first day of the New Year, finally becoming Elden Lord with a friend's help. You looked calm in front of the screen at the time, but inside you were already overwhelmed by memory. The people who've played games with you over the years have changed, again and again — and among them are some you cherish more than almost anything. Some people's gentleness sits right on the surface; other people's gentleness you only see if you look twice. When the call ends, you put down the controller, turn off the screen, and throw yourself heavily onto the bed. The withdrawal that follows finally makes you understand: it was never really about the game — it was about the person next to you. The people you want to see are either hundreds or tens of thousands of kilometres away, but now even a one-hour flight is something you hesitate over for a long time. You put away the urge to buy a plane ticket, and instead silently slide open the balcony door again, looking out at the endless snow.</p><p>"It would be nice if humans didn't have feelings. It would be nice if I didn't have feelings." You've thought this more than once — that maybe then you wouldn't hurt so much.</p><p>No one tells a person in their twenties what they're supposed to do, and no one tells them how to solve the pain of being in their twenties. Just like the two CVs and the portfolio sitting in front of you — you don't know where your future actually is either.</p><p>To use your own metaphor: right now you feel like you've just reached the bottom of the Elden Ring DLC for the first time, lost in a fog with no visible end. Except in the game you can place a marker to find your way — in real life, faced with that same fog, you have no idea which direction is even yours.</p><p>Always mistaken for being overly sentimental, you stay silent as always, as if there's a sheet of acrylic standing between you and the world.</p><p>"The moment personal feelings get mixed in, you feel like you can't do anything right."</p><p>As you've gained more experience, you've come to realize that having too much feeling isn't necessarily a good thing, so you deliberately hide the feelings that overflow, and every night, alone, you let them consume you. You bury the impulses that are about to break through your chest, and in places where no one can see, you turn them into words. Hundreds of thousands of words of real feeling — including the things you never got to say because of regret — are all carefully sealed into jars of memory, and then carelessly tossed into corners no one bothers to notice. You pull out half of your own soul and entrust it to those words. No draft needed, no real thinking required — these words seem to come as naturally to you as an inborn gift; you barely have time to catch your breath before they've already flowed out onto the page like water. That sleepless night, you got up and listened to David Tao, and found that songs you couldn't understand a decade ago now reveal a second layer of meaning in their lyrics. Mimicking the way ordinary people smoke, you bite down on a Pocky stick after stick, slide open the balcony door, and are met once again by the cool night air of daylight saving summer. Just like every sleepless night, once you finish the last bite of the biscuit in your hand, you walk back into your room and, as always, coax yourself to sleep. You keep stubbornly pushing people away, yet you long for the very people you pushed away to turn around, the next second, and hug you.</p><p>"Why are the boundaries of human feeling always so blurred?" You've thought about this unanswerable question more than once.</p><p>You try to keep yourself busy, telling yourself that this way you can forget all your troubles.</p><p>You seem to have so much, and yet in the end you find you have nothing at all.</p><p>"The distance between people should be like the distance between people and cats — you only get closer when you need to."</p><p>A cat can act spoiled whenever it wants. You can't.</p><p>"If falling asleep meant not hurting anymore, I'd rather just stay asleep forever."</p><p>You've always felt your relationships are a complete mess. You don't know whether the person you want to see even wants to see you — that person has their own world, and in the end you're just an outsider. All you can do is step back, or stay exactly where you are. There have been countless times, over countless things, when you've thought about ending it all — thinking maybe the world wouldn't be any different with one more or one less of you in it. But somehow, you're still here.</p><p>Because you love it too much, it's hard to say it out loud.</p><p>[Looks like, in the end, you gave your heart to the wrong person after all.]</p><p>However extroverted you look on the surface, that's how introverted you are underneath. So you've slowly come to understand that not every effort gets rewarded, and not every question has an answer. Maybe what you were chasing in the first place was a paradox to begin with. You often marvel at how your memory gets spent on the strangest things — you remember countless tiny, forgotten details that no one else would ever care about. You sometimes dream of the times in middle school when you were called to the math teacher's office, or scolded by your homeroom teacher. Maybe those three dark years left more of a shadow on your personality than you realize. You've always believed some things are bound to change with time, but reality doesn't seem to work that way. Even a wound that has bled will heal one day, but the scar it leaves behind still brands you, again and again, with the memory of that old pain.</p><p>"So do you hate them?"</p><p>"Of course. I will never forgive them, not in this lifetime — because everyone was an accomplice."</p><p>Slowly you realize that being able to listen without judging is genuinely a hard thing to do.</p><p>Learning to make peace with yourself is, for you, no easier than passing a math exam. But it really is one of your life's assignments.</p><p>And yet, even so, you still long to be reunited with that person under the romantic light and shadow of early autumn, to hold their hand and hear them say those words to you, in person.</p><p>It was only later, belatedly, that you realized: "You're not alone anymore" — that's a sentence only you will ever say to yourself.</p><p>You wake up and realize everything you just saw was only a dream. You're not as devastated as you expected — you just pick up your phone and look again at that empty chat window. The last message is still frozen from eight days ago: "Maybe I talk too much." The feeling of not deserving things grows heavier in your chest, and you realize you were never as strong as you imagined. So you turn off your phone, walk into the shower, turn on the speaker, and play that same familiar playlist. From "Night Changes" to 「変わりゆくもの変わらないもの」— hundreds of hours of songs still can't tell the whole of your few decades of life. Standing under the water, you laugh at your own stubbornness, because you still love the things you loved ten years ago — including the person you loved ten years ago. You cherish every person who matters to you, and you long to be cherished the same way in return. But you also know that's nothing more than a fantasy — those good things were never going to be yours. So what, exactly, is the price of growing up? You grip the printed pages of something you wrote, and the next second, without hesitation, tear them apart and throw them in the trash. That handkerchief that smelled of cool mint, long discontinued; that copy of Deng Shi (Dad, I Want You to Know), its pages yellowed and brittle; that box of letters and postcards, full of memory, locked away in a wooden trunk; that essay you edited over and over, still only ever an A-; that evening lecture on Imposter Syndrome; that mildly depressive high summer that lives on in your memory; the excitement of the first time you stepped into fandom writing; the disappointment of an offer that came too late; that bus you missed; the blue sky and the giant sculpture you saw at the Montreal art museum. The things once etched into your blood are still there, even now, and they still walk with you. All these short pages of long-held feeling still can't hold the whole of your twenty-some years. You suddenly remember being asked once what you wished for, and without thinking, blurting out that vague answer: "To stay with the person I love, forever." Only after you'd said it did you realize it was nothing but an empty wish. You start to regret it, asking yourself why you weren't better to the person you loved, so you close every social app, curl yourself up, and dive once more into that unreal world, even knowing they don't actually exist. But their story is your story, their regrets are your regrets — it's just that the real feeling that moved you so much, you may never get the chance to have in this life. Through the mist, you see him lying beside him, still that same eighteen-year-old, still wearing that same faint smile. After all, the only thing that has ever moved you is a boy who never ages — no one stays young forever, but there's always someone who does. Year after year, you sigh that you're getting older, that you feel less and less capable in so many things. Just like every new document you open, still always Times New Roman, 12pt, double-spaced — you keep blaming yourself, always believing your own stubbornness has caused, directly or indirectly, so many mistakes. It would be nice if you simply didn't exist, you think — then none of this would ever have...</p><p>But then again — what was that answer you spent your whole life chasing, anyway?</p><p>But in your heart you hear a voice telling you to turn around. You turn, and find her still standing behind you, just as she was ten years ago, as if she'd never left. Setting aside your usual guarded pride, maybe some part of you still longs for that innocence from ten years ago — longs for the faintly humid, sweltering air of that summer, that first brand-new subway line, some early morning, a steaming cappuccino from Starbucks and a chocolate muffin fresh out of the oven. So you reach out your hand, and she gently takes it in return. You look at her, unable to believe it, and she looks back at you, just as she did ten years ago. She nods at you, a silent kind of comfort. You understand you're still longing, just as fiercely as before, only this time it's for an uncertain future of your own making. Yes — this world really should sing for you, and for idealists like him.</p><p>So you sit back down in front of the screen, and start typing again, writing a preface for your own piece — except this time, you add one more line.</p><p>"This piece is dedicated to the entire graduating class of 2025."</p></div></div></div><p><a href="/en/writing/">← Back to Writing</a></p></article></Base>

<script is:inline>
  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', () => {
  document.querySelectorAll('.ep-layout').forEach((layout) => {
    const tabs = layout.querySelectorAll('.ep-tab');
    const panels = layout.querySelectorAll('.ep-panel');
    const orderedTabs = [...tabs].sort((a, b) => Number(a.textContent.match(/\d+/)?.[0]) - Number(b.textContent.match(/\d+/)?.[0]));
    const tabContainer = layout.querySelector('.ep-tabs');
    const panelContainer = layout.querySelector('.ep-panels');
    orderedTabs.forEach((tab) => tabContainer.appendChild(tab));
    orderedTabs.forEach((tab) => panelContainer.appendChild(layout.querySelector('.ep-panel[data-ep="' + tab.dataset.ep + '"]')));
    const getStats = (panel) => {
      const text = [...panel.querySelectorAll('p')].map((paragraph) => paragraph.textContent).join(' ');
      const words = (text.match(/\S+/g) || []).length;
      const minutes = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(words / 200));
      return `${words.toLocaleString('en-US')} words · about ${minutes} min read`;
    };
    orderedTabs.forEach((tab) => {
      const panel = layout.querySelector('.ep-panel[data-ep="' + tab.dataset.ep + '"]');
      const stats = getStats(panel);
      const panelTitle = document.createElement('h2');
      panelTitle.className = 'ep-title';
      panelTitle.textContent = tab.textContent.trim();
      const panelMeta = document.createElement('div');
      panelMeta.className = 'ep-reading-meta';
      panelMeta.textContent = `${stats} · Musings`;
      panel.prepend(panelMeta);
      panel.prepend(panelTitle);
    });
    tabs.forEach((tab) => {
      tab.addEventListener('click', () => {
        tabs.forEach((t) => t.classList.remove('active'));
        panels.forEach((p) => p.classList.remove('active', 'entering'));
        tab.classList.add('active');
        const panel = layout.querySelector('.ep-panel[data-ep="' + tab.dataset.ep + '"]');
        panel.classList.add('active');
        window.requestAnimationFrame(() => panel.classList.add('entering'));
        panels[0].closest('.ep-panels').scrollTop = 0;
      });
    });
  });
});
</script>

<style>
  .prose:has(.ep-layout) { max-width:1000px; }
  .ep-layout { margin-top:24px; display:grid; grid-template-columns:230px 1fr; gap:36px; align-items:start; }
  .ep-tabs { display:flex; flex-direction:column; position:sticky; top:24px; }
  .ep-tab { text-align:left; background:none; border:none; border-left:2px solid var(--line); padding:11px 16px; font:inherit; font-size:.93rem; color:var(--muted); cursor:pointer; line-height:1.4; transition:color .18s ease,border-color .18s ease; }
  .ep-tab:hover { color:var(--ink); }
  .ep-tab.active { border-left-color:var(--rose); color:var(--ink); font-weight:500; }
  .ep-panels { min-width:0; }
  .ep-panel { display:none; gap:12px; max-width:100%; }
  .ep-panel.active { display:grid; }
  .ep-title { margin:0 0 2px; font-size:clamp(1.8rem,3.5vw,3rem); line-height:1.2; font-weight:500; letter-spacing:-.035em; }
  .ep-reading-meta { color:var(--muted); font: .72rem ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif; letter-spacing:.04em; margin-bottom:2px; opacity:.85; }
  .ep-panel.entering { animation: ep-panel-in .52s cubic-bezier(.2,.7,.2,1) both; }
  .ep-panel.entering p { animation: ep-copy-in .55s ease both; }
  .ep-panel.entering p:nth-child(2) { animation-delay:70ms; } .ep-panel.entering p:nth-child(3) { animation-delay:105ms; } .ep-panel.entering p:nth-child(4) { animation-delay:140ms; } .ep-panel.entering p:nth-child(5) { animation-delay:175ms; }
  .ep-panel p { margin:0; color:var(--muted); font-size:.96rem; line-height:1.8; }
  .ep-panel p.scene-break { text-align:center; letter-spacing:.3em; color:var(--rose); opacity:.7; margin:0; }
  @keyframes ep-panel-in { from { opacity:0; transform:translateY(12px); clip-path:inset(0 0 8% 0); } to { opacity:1; transform:none; clip-path:inset(0); } }
  @keyframes ep-copy-in { from { opacity:0; transform:translateY(8px); } to { opacity:1; transform:none; } }
  @media (max-width:760px) { .ep-layout { grid-template-columns:1fr; } .ep-tabs { flex-direction:row; flex-wrap:wrap; position:static; gap:4px 14px; } .ep-tab { border-left:none; border-bottom:2px solid var(--line); padding:6px 2px; } .ep-tab.active { border-bottom-color:var(--rose); } }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce) { .ep-panel.entering, .ep-panel.entering p { animation:none; } }
</style>
